<HelpLayout title="Moving to a new phone" description="How to carry your Badger workout history, routines and settings to a new phone, from Cloud backup or a backup file.">
  <h1>Moving to a new phone</h1>

  <p>To move Badger to a new phone, restore a backup on it. If <a href={`/help/settings/#cloud-backup`}>Cloud backup</a> was on, restore from your Google Drive. Otherwise, make a backup file on the old phone and copy it across yourself. The steps are the same on Android and iPhone.</p>

  <h2>What moves across</h2>
  <p>A backup holds everything in Badger:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Every workout and set, with warmups, comments, RPE and RIR.</li>
    <li>Your exercise library, including custom exercises and categories.</li>
    <li>All routines, days and planned sets.</li>
    <li>Body measurements and body-weight history.</li>
    <li>Gyms, machines, attachments, bars, accessories and their links to exercises, plus each gym's plates and racks.</li>
    <li>Your settings (accent color, weight unit, theme, notifications) and your training notes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Photos of your machines, attachments and bars are included only when <strong>Back up equipment photos</strong> is on; see <a href={`/help/settings/#cloud-backup`}>Cloud backup</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="cloud-backup">Restore from Cloud backup</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Install Badger on the new phone and go through the short onboarding.</li>
    <li>Go to <strong>More → Settings → Cloud backup</strong>.</li>
    <li>Turn on <strong>Auto backup to your Drive</strong> and sign in with the same Google account as before.</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>Restore from cloud…</strong>, pick a backup and confirm.</li>
    <li>Restart the app when it finishes.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Move with a backup file</h2>

  <h3>Step 1: Back up on the old phone</h3>
  <p>Do this after your last workout on the old phone. Anything you log after the backup won't come across.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Go to <strong>More → Settings → Your data</strong>.</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>Manual backup</strong> to open the share sheet.</li>
    <li>Save the <code>.workout_backup</code> file somewhere the new phone can reach: Google Drive, an email to yourself, a messaging app, a USB transfer or your cloud storage app.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Step 2: Install Badger on the new phone</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Install Badger from the App Store or Google Play.</li>
    <li>Go through the short onboarding. Skip the import step at the end; you'll restore in Step 3.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Step 3: Restore the backup</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Make sure the backup file is on the new phone (saved in Files or Drive, or attached to an email you've opened).</li>
    <li>In Badger, go to <strong>More → Settings → Your data</strong>.</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>Restore from backup</strong> and pick the <code>.workout_backup</code> file.</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>Restore</strong> to confirm. Badger replaces its data with the backup and restarts, with all your history in place.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <strong>Restoring replaces what's on the phone.</strong> Anything you've already logged on the new phone is lost. If you're unsure, make a manual backup of it first. Badger also keeps a copy of what it replaced; see <a href={`/help/settings/#backup`}>Restore previous data</a>.
  </div>

  <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The picker doesn't see my file.</strong> Move the backup into your phone's main <em>Downloads</em> or <em>Documents</em> folder, or open it from your cloud app's share sheet ("Open with Badger").</li>
    <li><strong>Restore fails with a format error.</strong> The file was probably cut short in transfer. Make a new backup on the old phone and move it a different way.</li>
    <li><strong>The old phone is dead and there's no backup file.</strong> If Cloud backup was on, <a href={`#cloud-backup`}>restore from it</a>. If it was off and there's no backup file, the data can't be recovered.</li>
  </ul>
</HelpLayout>
